      source: "Original",
      prompt: `<div class="doc-block"><p class="doc-label">Secondary Source Excerpt</p><p>"To call this period simply an age of unprecedented prosperity is to see only the surface. It was, more precisely, an age in which prosperity for some was built directly upon conditions of genuine hardship for others — the same railroad network that connected a nation and enriched its builders also charged the farmers who depended on it rates many could scarcely afford."</p><p class="doc-source">— Adapted from a historian's characterization of the Gilded Age (illustrative excerpt)</p></div>`,
